```java
class Settings {
    private final Map<String, String> values;

    Settings(Map<String, String> values) {
        this.values = Map.copyOf(values);   // defensive copy, immutable
    }

    String get(String key) { return values.get(key); }
}

// publication:
static final Settings SETTINGS = new Settings(loadFromDisk());
```

Because `values` is `final` and assigned entirely inside the constructor, and `SETTINGS` itself is published through a `static final` field, the JVM's initialization guarantees ensure any thread that observes `SETTINGS` sees the fully-constructed `Settings` object, including its fully-populated map. No explicit lock needed anywhere in this path.

```java
class Settings {
    static Settings LATEST;   // not final — the bug
    private Map<String, String> values;

    Settings(Map<String, String> values) {
        LATEST = this;              // 'this' escapes before the field below is set!
        this.values = Map.copyOf(values);
    }
}
```

Assigning `LATEST = this` inside the constructor, before `values` is set, publishes the reference before the object is fully built — another thread reading `LATEST` at exactly that moment can see a `Settings` instance whose `values` field is still `null`. This is called **letting `this` escape** during construction, and it's unsafe regardless of whether the field is later made final.
